@prefix chg: <https://semblance.dev/ns/change#> .
@prefix mig: <https://semblance.dev/ns/migration#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix change: <https://semblance.dev/change/> .
@prefix c: <https://semblance.dev/ns/component#> .
@prefix sem: <https://semblance.dev/ns/core#> .
@prefix l10n: <https://semblance.dev/ns/l10n#> .

# What changed for whoever has to move with it: AGENTS.md, "When a component changes".

change:texts-picked-as-they-arrive a chg:Change ;
    rdfs:label "A text's translations are picked in the change that brings them, member by member"@en ;
    chg:issue <https://github.com/inferal-oss/semblance/issues/589> ;
    chg:motivation """A SPARQL rule reads a translated text as its twin (l10n:Terms), else the one value it has. While
graphs arrived in slices (the graphs a page did not need first), l10n:Terms waited for the last slice, since it was
evaluated over the whole graph; until then a node that had arrived with its translations had no twin, its fallback
bound all of them, and the page showed whichever the store gave last: the Tools window's sample queries in Arabic,
for seconds, to an English reader.""" ;
    chg:substance """sem:watchesManyValued now makes a rendering member-wise over the nodes it says something about: those
with two values or more of a marked predicate in the input, which the engine keeps as changes arrive. Each node's pair
is evaluated in the change that gives it a second value, again when its values change, withdrawn when it is left with
one, and every pair again when the viewer's languages change; nothing waits for the end of an arrival. l10n:Terms is
written that way: per node ($member), its twins where they were (<viewer>/~t-<md5 of the node>, l10n:of the node). The picks are the same, the viewer's languages by rank, then the untagged value, then English,
then the least. Its SPARQL rule takes many nodes in one query (VALUES ?member). Runtime.arriving no longer holds
anything back in the engine.""" ;
    chg:forwardGuide """Nothing to move for a rule that reads a twin (`?at l10n:of ?n ; l10n:name ?picked`): its node and
its value are as before, and arrive sooner. A rendering of your own with sem:watchesManyValued is now member-wise: read
the node as $member, and have each node it writes name it (`?node ?p $member`) so its pairs are evaluated in bulk.""" ;
    chg:backwardGuide """Going back, a graph arriving in slices has its translated texts picked only after the last
slice: until then a SPARQL rule may show any of their values.""" ;
    chg:affects l10n:Viewer .
